/* book Worked example 2: Q: A bracket for a coastal pump house can be mild steel, aluminium alloy 6061-T6 or stainless steel 304. Data: yield strength 250, 276 and 215 MPa; E 200, 69 and 193 GPa; cost ₹60, ₹300 and ₹250 per kg; corrosion rating out of 5: 1, 4 and 5. Weights: strength 0.25, stiffness 0.15, cost 0.35, corrosion 0.25. Rank the materials by the weighted-point method.
   1. Scaled strength (best 276): mild steel 100 × 250/276 = 90.6; aluminium 100; stainless 100 × 215/276 = 77.9.
   2. Scaled stiffness (best 200): 100; 100 × 69/200 = 34.5; 100 × 193/200 = 96.5.
   3. Scaled cost (lowest ₹60): 100; 100 × 60/300 = 20.0; 100 × 60/250 = 24.0.
   4. Scaled corrosion (best 5): 100 × 1/5 = 20; 80; 100.
   5. Mild steel: 0.25 × 90.6 + 0.15 × 100 + 0.35 × 100 + 0.25 × 20 = 22.65 + 15 + 35 + 5 = 77.6.
   6. Aluminium: 0.25 × 100 + 0.15 × 34.5 + 0.35 × 20 + 0.25 × 80 = 25 + 5.18 + 7 + 20 = 57.2. Stainless: 0.25 × 77.9 + 0.15 × 96.5 + 0.35 × 24 + 0.25 × 100 = 19.48 + 14.48 + 8.4 + 25 = 67.3.
   Answer: Mild steel 77.6, stainless 67.3, aluminium 57.2. Mild steel wins on cost, but only if a protective coating (galvanising or paint) is specified for the salt air; if coating is not allowed, the corrosion screen removes it and stainless steel is chosen. */
