/* book exam traps: Raising strength to cut deflection. Heat treatment raises Syt but not E, so a stiffness-limited steel part does not get stiffer. | Designing a ductile part on Sut, or a brittle part on a yield strength it does not have. Grey cast iron has no clear yield point. | Confusing hardness with toughness. A hardened file is hard but brittle; a mild steel hook is soft but tough. | Comparing materials by strength alone. Size the part in each, then compare mass and cost, as in the tie-rod example. | Reading 40C8 as 40 % carbon. It is 0.40 % carbon and about 0.8 % manganese. */
